 ## Overview
-This method addresses the inconsistency of single-view 2D inpainting in 3D scenes by introducing a training view selection mechanism that leverages multi-view correlations [S1]. It distinguishes itself from pure 2D inpainting approaches by dynamically deciding whether to rely on a pretrained 2D inpainter's appearance prior or to reconstruct the occluded area using supplementary information from other visible views, based on the texture richness of the occluded regions [S1].
+Adaptive View Selection for Inpainting is a method for 3D scene de-occlusion and inpainting that integrates a 2D image inpainter with a NeRF optimization loop to recover occluded regions while maintaining multi-view consistency [S1]. It addresses the limitation of single 2D inpainters, which ignore 3D geometric consistency, by dynamically selecting whether to rely on 2D appearance priors or supplementary information from other training views based on local texture richness [S1]. Unlike inference-time interception methods [DFF (Decomposing NeRF for Editing Via Feature Field Distillation)](12068-dff-decomposing-nerf-for-editing-via-feature-field-distillat.md), this approach actively updates the NeRF parameters $\Theta$ using a reconstruction loss that adaptively weights 2D priors against multi-view geometric constraints [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Inputs:** A pre-trained NeRF representation $F_\Theta$ (mapping $\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{d} \to \sigma, \mathbf{c}$), a set of input views with known camera poses, a binary mask $M$ identifying the occluded object in the reference view, and a pretrained 2D image inpainter (specifically LaMa [S1]).
-*   **Outputs:** A modified NeRF representation that realistically inpaints the occluded area, ensuring multi-view consistency.
-
